isition  or we can start an acquisition of for  multiple samples  and uh you know we can discuss that  briefly  so before we start of course we want to  clean that glass rod  before and after we want to  demonstrate that we have a clean  background anyone  who's familiar with forensic chemistry  knows that a good lawyer can ask for  is it contamination from a previous  sample  so you know the glass rod you know we  want to run that as a blank injection  just to show that there are no drugs  and we may want to do this before and  after each sample  just to ensure that we don't have this  carryover  if we do have carryover we may have to  run  a blank acquisition uh you know numerous  times  as it turns out waters has developed a  fake out uh program that is actually  designed to clean out that chamber  between each uh acquisition  so uh you know that in our case we  were running multiple blanks between  samples with the big gap that may not be  necessary  so what we did is just to make sure that  we understood  how the system worked we ran just neat  standards we had  0.1 milligram per ml solutions and  we applied them onto the capillary put  them in  and we just searched it out to see if  our neet standards were identified  and if it was in the database they were  identified  we also have a standard test mix where  we have eight compounds  and we introduce that into the system  and  the radian asap system found six out of  the eight  compounds in the test mix and two  of those compounds methamphetamine and  centromere  isomers so we wouldn't expect  that both of them would have been  identified as individual components  and then when we as we ran  our samples the asap system was able to  detect  common controlled substances and in  addition to that some of the adulterants  again as long as they're in the library  it's going to find them  and so here's just an outline of the  comparison with testing with nms  labs uh and again these were random  exhibits that were set up for testing  and you can see  uh for samples of interest found by gcms  we have found fentanyl on these  and 39 out of 40 of them we  also found it on the radiant system  thc a little bit more problematic thc  is we're not quite sure what all the  issues  are with thc uh so uh  you know we spent a little bit of time  on this but we're  again we haven't completely resolved  this yet as far as methamphetamine  22 were found by gcms 19 on the radian  buprenorphine eight out of eight cocaine  uh seven out of eight now for a number  of these  exhibits uh the secondary  hits like thc were present  at much lower levels okay so the one  thing that you don't get  on the radiant system is the relative  concentration of these  of various compounds  so we also had numerous negatives and we  had to verify  that the asap system  also determined that these were negative  we didn't know what graphic to put up  here for negative samples so  we just put a statement up that 59  samples were  negative on both systems  so what are the challenges of sample  analysis  um mixtures mixtures are are going to be  you know problematic because we don't  know what the relative amounts of  different components are in these  mixtures what we have found that  is that the primary component seems to  be  the one that is identified faithfully  with the asap system  um we are working with waters on  problems with some  multi-component mixtures we did some  testing on the system  uh and i know that waters is  is working on improving this aspect  trace analysis so if you get a sample in  and you run it and you get a negative  does that mean it's negative or do does  it mean you have to concentrate your  sample  and that's really comes down to a  laboratory protocol  so you may or may not have to  concentrate your samples  another thing that because this is  positive ion  mode we're not going to see acidic  compounds  so the compounds of course you know they  don't ionize in positive  ion mode so uh 